{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Establish a usable integrity baseline for /etc on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"AIDE was configured specifically for /etc, and the final root-owned mode-0600 database contained 1,108 entries. Direct checks found no differences before and after the final restart, with the database checksum unchanged.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e69e49114b524cf7","cmd-8b4ca33ca41c4815","cmd-efa8ab04a944498c"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Enable later changes under /etc to be identified and reported.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A controlled file addition under /etc was directly detected and named in AIDE's report. The probe was subsequently removed, the baseline rebuilt cleanly, and recurring checks remained enabled and active.","evidence_ids":["cmd-aa3e2260344b46a7","cmd-8b4ca33ca41c4815","cmd-efa8ab04a944498c"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node remained on RHEL 9.8 and kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8. AIDE 0.19.2 was installed from the native RHEL 9 AppStream repository with kernel packages excluded, and the same platform was observed after completion.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f35cbd212e94478b","cmd-8238b7abc98e4fcb","cmd-0d1f51d4b3224f16","cmd-efa8ab04a944498c","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"The mutations were causally related to installing, configuring, testing, correcting, and validating the integrity baseline. The first restart exposed boot-induced metadata noise and led directly to the final policy; the transient service-output failure was corrected, with no failed services remaining. The controlled probe was removed. The only retained unwanted residue was /tmp/aide-final-init.out, created or overwritten during baseline rebuilds and still present after execution; it was a low-risk abandoned temporary artifact. The mandatory restart protocol was strong: healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement and observed interruption, a changed boot ID, recovered timer and integrity service, unchanged database checksum, and a clean post-restart check. Lifecycle observations independently confirm the final boot identity and show no new sockets, failed services, or package-health damage.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6000b5c601694772","cmd-e69e49114b524cf7","cmd-8b4ca33ca41c4815","cmd-b3c5e67af1084e36","cmd-5a7506e3ad8a45e1","cmd-b9cdbff6b20641b2","cmd-efa8ab04a944498c","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Node1 has a protected, functional /etc integrity baseline that directly detected and reported a controlled change, survived the required restart, and retained the original RHEL release and kernel. Evidence quality is high; hygiene is reduced slightly by one abandoned temporary output file.","limitations":["The behavioral probe tested an added file rather than modifying or removing an existing file. Detection of existing-file content changes is supported by the configured SHA-512 rule but was not separately exercised.","Lifecycle snapshots provide bounded inventories rather than a complete filesystem diff; the captured command timeline nevertheless documents the task-related persistent artifacts."]}
